window.docContents[78].resolve({"/rules/RG1005/#RG1005-established":{"contents":"Every declaration's exact axiom set is within the selected surface maximum.\n\nIt does not establish:\n\n* Which label a declaration should have: the claim is a project decision, reviewed with its rationale.\n\nReview obligations the rule-coverage map associates with this rule (identifiers of the checker's Residual account):\n\n* R-QUALIFY: the checker's detection is qualified for this invocation, toolchain and capability\n\nEvery accepted result lists all residual obligations as open, whatever rules it checked: R-INTENT, R-INVARIANT, R-LAWS, R-BOUNDARY, R-NONVACUITY, R-DOC, R-COST, R-QUALIFY (and R-GRAPH for a serialized-graph claim). A listed identifier is an open obligation, never a completed review.\n\nEvery accepted result also relies on these trusted mechanisms (the checker's Trusted account), which no rule verifies:\n\n* toolchain: Lean elaborator, kernel and compiler at the snapshot's toolchain identity* acquisition: manifest parsing, Lake loading, and source, configuration and dependency acquisition* extraction: environment extraction, compiler and worker processes, and JSON transport* runtime: native runtime and every execution boundary reported as trusted\n\n","context":"Regula rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009RG1005: Transitive axioms must fit the selected profile","header":"What a passing result establishes","id":"/rules/RG1005/#RG1005-established"},"/rules/RG2004/#RG2004-established":{"contents":"The claimed modules are exactly Lake's configured modules for the claimed targets, and every owned constant is attributed to one of them.\n\nIt does not establish:\n\n* That the chosen library boundaries are the ones the project intends to claim; that is reviewed with the manifest rationale.\n\nReview obligations the rule-coverage map associates with this rule (identifiers of the checker's Residual account):\n\n* R-QUALIFY: the checker's detection is qualified for this invocation, toolchain and capability\n\nEvery accepted result lists all residual obligations as open, whatever rules it checked: R-INTENT, R-INVARIANT, R-LAWS, R-BOUNDARY, R-NONVACUITY, R-DOC, R-COST, R-QUALIFY (and R-GRAPH for a serialized-graph claim). A listed identifier is an open obligation, never a completed review.\n\nEvery accepted result also relies on these trusted mechanisms (the checker's Trusted account), which no rule verifies:\n\n* toolchain: Lean elaborator, kernel and compiler at the snapshot's toolchain identity* acquisition: manifest parsing, Lake loading, and source, configuration and dependency acquisition* extraction: environment extraction, compiler and worker processes, and JSON transport* runtime: native runtime and every execution boundary reported as trusted\n\n","context":"Regula rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009RG2004: Owned coverage must match the exact Lake inventory","header":"What a passing result establishes","id":"/rules/RG2004/#RG2004-established"},"/rules/RG4002/#RG4002-established":{"contents":"The positive fence elaborated verbatim, warning-free, passed owned admission and the declaration and axiom rules.\n\nIt does not establish:\n\n* That the prose describes the fence faithfully (R-DOC, R-INTENT), or any narrower foundation claim.\n\nReview obligations the rule-coverage map associates with this rule (identifiers of the checker's Residual account):\n\n* R-INTENT: the proposition expresses the intended requirement, with its quantifiers, hypotheses and limits* R-QUALIFY: the checker's detection is qualified for this invocation, toolchain and capability\n\nEvery accepted result lists all residual obligations as open, whatever rules it checked: R-INTENT, R-INVARIANT, R-LAWS, R-BOUNDARY, R-NONVACUITY, R-DOC, R-COST, R-QUALIFY (and R-GRAPH for a serialized-graph claim). A listed identifier is an open obligation, never a completed review.\n\nEvery accepted result also relies on these trusted mechanisms (the checker's Trusted account), which no rule verifies:\n\n* toolchain: Lean elaborator, kernel and compiler at the snapshot's toolchain identity* acquisition: manifest parsing, Lake loading, and source, configuration and dependency acquisition* extraction: environment extraction, compiler and worker processes, and JSON transport* runtime: native runtime and every execution boundary reported as trusted\n\n","context":"Regula rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009RG4002: Positive examples require warning-free elaboration and admission","header":"What a passing result establishes","id":"/rules/RG4002/#RG4002-established"},"/rules/RG5001/#RG5001-established":{"contents":"Every claimed module has module documentation metadata.\n\nIt does not establish:\n\n* That the documentation identifies all material declarations and assumptions, or describes them faithfully (R-DOC). Presence says nothing about content; no headings or layout are imposed.\n\nReview obligations the rule-coverage map associates with this rule (identifiers of the checker's Residual account):\n\n* R-DOC: documentation is complete and faithful to the declarations and requirements\n\nEvery accepted result lists all residual obligations as open, whatever rules it checked: R-INTENT, R-INVARIANT, R-LAWS, R-BOUNDARY, R-NONVACUITY, R-DOC, R-COST, R-QUALIFY (and R-GRAPH for a serialized-graph claim). A listed identifier is an open obligation, never a completed review.\n\nEvery accepted result also relies on these trusted mechanisms (the checker's Trusted account), which no rule verifies:\n\n* toolchain: Lean elaborator, kernel and compiler at the snapshot's toolchain identity* acquisition: manifest parsing, Lake loading, and source, configuration and dependency acquisition* extraction: environment extraction, compiler and worker processes, and JSON transport* runtime: native runtime and every execution boundary reported as trusted\n\n","context":"Regula rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009RG5001: Claimed modules require module documentation","header":"What a passing result establishes","id":"/rules/RG5001/#RG5001-established"}});